On 9 November 2026 a group of governments, AI companies and research and civil-society groups put their names to a short declaration on the safety of the most capable AI models. It goes by several names: Canada calls it a joint declaration on the Paris call, the Netherlands calls it the Paris Declaration, and the Elysee in France calls it the Paris Call for AI Safety and Security. This post explains what the text says, who signed, and what it does not say.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording. This is not a full human review. Anthropic is listed as a company signatory of the declaration, and OpenAI and Microsoft are listed too, so the author is not a neutral party. Stackbrook did not test any product mentioned and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not financial or legal advice.*

## The short version

![Flow diagram of the three commitments in the Paris Call for AI Safety and Security: evaluate, be accountable, share warnings](/images/site1/paris-ai-safety-declaration-explained-what-it-commits-to-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages. It restates the text in plain words; it is not an official graphic.*

The declaration is aimed at "frontier" AI models, the term the text uses for the most advanced systems (the text does not define it). Its stated starting point is that such models cross national borders, so their risks and benefits are global and no single actor can manage them alone. It then sets out three commitments, covered below. The text does not state any new obligation for ordinary businesses that use AI tools. It does show where rules and vendor promises may be heading.

## The three commitments

**First, a common way to evaluate frontier models.** The text calls for a shared framework for testing and managing risk, building on frameworks that already exist. It points to common technical standards and testing methods, with a focus on dangerous misuse (including cyber and chemical, biological, radiological and nuclear areas) and on the risk of models acting against human intent, including loss of control. It points to networks such as the International Network for Advanced AI Measurement, Evaluation and Science as a way to make evaluation results easier to compare across regions. It also calls for publishing the non-sensitive findings of evaluations in a standard format and creating a publicly accessible shared repository, which could hold items such as red-teaming protocols and stress-test results.

**Second, responsibility for everyone in the AI chain.** Signatories commit to comply with safety standards set or recognized by national authorities, with independent third-party evaluation. Policy makers commit to clarifying legal responsibility for large-scale harm, including cross-border harm, and to giving AI companies legal certainty. Frontier developers commit to several things: disclosing model capabilities, limitations and known risks; supporting independent evaluators where appropriate; building fail-safe mechanisms to shut down model operations within their control (the text also mentions restrictions and pauses), including during training and internal use; and running internal governance with continuous risk management and red-teaming. The text also acknowledges the risk of safeguards being removed or bypassed, while saying the benefits of open-weight development should be preserved.

**Third, early warning and information sharing.** The signatories commit to rapid alert systems that build on existing incident-response arrangements, such as those used in cybersecurity, and to aligning how incidents are classified. That includes sharing timely information on serious cyber incidents and "AI control" incidents among companies and governments. Policy makers also commit to secure, confidential channels for whistleblowers raising concerns about systemic risk, without fear of legal or professional retaliation. On a possible permanent cooperation mechanism, the text only says the signatories will explore whether one would help and what its scope could be.

## Who signed

The three official pages we read do not list exactly the same signatories, and Canada and the Elysee both say the list is open to new endorsements and still being updated. The company signatories that appear on all three pages are Anthropic, Black Forest Labs, Cohere, Domyn, Hugging Face, Microsoft, Mistral AI, OpenAI and Synthesia. Some pages add others, such as Sakana AI and Credo AI. Bahrain, Canada, Finland, France, Norway and the Netherlands appear as government signatories on all three. Beyond those six, the government lists differ: the Netherlands page also names Japan, Sierra Leone and the United Kingdom, while the Elysee page names a much longer list of European countries and Kenya. Because the lists conflict, check the current version of an official page before relying on any specific country being a signatory.

## What the text does not say

This part matters most for anyone tempted to treat the declaration as a rulebook. On the pages we read, there are no deadlines or review dates, and no named secretariat or lead body. None of the pages states whether the declaration is legally binding. It does not define "frontier model", does not name the national authorities whose standards would apply, and does not describe how the standard reporting format or the shared repository would work or who would host it. The text sets out no mechanism for verifying compliance and no stated consequence for failing to follow it, although the Dutch and Elysee versions list verification among the areas of consensus. This post relies only on the three government pages listed in the sources.

## What it means for a small team

For a business that simply uses AI tools, the practical effect today is indirect. If these commitments are followed, vendors could publish clearer information on risks and limits and report serious incidents more consistently. Things a team using AI tools might look at in the meantime include whether a vendor publishes a model card or safety policy, and which tools are in use and what data each can reach.

Our earlier guide on [what to check before giving an AI tool your customer data](/posts/ai-tool-customer-data-checklist) covers related questions in more detail.

## The takeaway

The Paris Call is a joint statement by governments, companies and research and civil-society groups with three clear themes: test frontier models in a shared way, spell out who is responsible when things go wrong, and share warnings quickly. As of 20 November 2026 the published text leaves out dates, enforcement and legal force, and the signatory lists differ between official pages. Watch for the first published evaluation format, any named host for the shared repository, and any move from declaration to law. Until then, the text reads as a signal of direction and does not state a requirement.
